---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["are-breaks-paid"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    In the US, <strong>short breaks are paid and meal breaks usually
    aren't</strong>. Under federal law, rest breaks of about 20 minutes or less
    count as work time. A genuine meal break of 30 minutes or more, where
    you're completely relieved of duty, doesn't have to be paid. Federal law
    doesn't require employers to give breaks at all, but some states do,
    California above all. In Canada and the UK, required breaks are generally
    unpaid unless your contract says otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>United States: the federal rule</h2>
  <p>The Fair Labor Standards Act doesn't require breaks, but if they're given:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Break</th><th>Paid?</th><th>Condition</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Rest break, usually 5–20 minutes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Counts as hours worked, including toward overtime</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Meal break, usually 30 minutes or more</td><td>No</td><td>Only if you're completely relieved of duty</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Meal break where you keep working</td><td>Yes</td><td>Eating at your desk while answering calls is work time</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The Department of Labor's guidance says rest periods of 20 minutes or
    less "are customarily paid for as working time", while bona fide meal
    periods of typically 30 minutes or more "generally need not be
    compensated". An employer can't call a 10-minute break a "meal" to avoid
    paying for it.
  </p>

  <h2>California: required breaks and penalty pay</h2>
  <p>California requires breaks for most non-exempt employees:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Meal period:</strong> a 30-minute unpaid meal break before the
      end of the fifth hour of work. It can be waived by mutual consent if the
      whole workday is 6 hours or less. A second meal break is required after
      10 hours, with its own waiver rules.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rest periods:</strong> 10 minutes of paid rest for every 4 hours
      worked "or major fraction thereof". None is required if the workday is
      under 3&frac12; hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Premium pay:</strong> if a required meal or rest break isn't
      provided, the employer owes one extra hour of pay at the employee's
      regular rate for each workday it happens (one premium for meals and one
      for rest breaks, at most).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    "Major fraction" means more than half, so the number of paid rest
    breaks steps up at 3&frac12;, 6, 10 and 14 hours:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Hours worked in the day</th><th>Paid 10-minute rest breaks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Under 3.5</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.5 up to 6</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>More than 6, up to 10</td><td>2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>More than 10, up to 14</td><td>3</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Several other states also require meal or rest breaks, with different
    timings and exceptions. Check your state labor department's site.
  </p>

  <h2>Canada (Ontario)</h2>
  <p>
    Ontario's Employment Standards Act requires a
    <strong>30-minute eating period after every 5 hours</strong> of work. It
    can be split into two breaks if you and your employer agree. Eating
    periods are unpaid unless your contract says they're paid, but if you're
    required to work or stay on call during the break, that time is work.
    Other provinces have their own rules; federally regulated workplaces
    follow the Canada Labour Code.
  </p>

  <h2>United Kingdom</h2>
  <p>
    Adult workers have the right to one uninterrupted
    <strong>20-minute rest break</strong> during the working day if they work
    more than 6 hours. GOV.UK notes that the break doesn't have to be paid;
    it depends on the employment contract. The 20 minutes should be a single
    break taken during the day, not at the start or end. Workers also get 11
    hours' rest between working days. Young workers have stronger rights.
  </p>

  <h2>Australia</h2>
  <p>
    In Australia, breaks are set by the modern award or enterprise agreement
    that covers you rather than by a single national rule. Many awards
    include paid rest breaks and an unpaid meal break after a set number of
    hours. Check your award on the Fair Work Ombudsman's website.
  </p>

  <h2>How breaks change your hours and pay</h2>
  <p>
    Only <strong>unpaid</strong> breaks are taken off your hours worked. Paid
    rest breaks stay in.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Example.</strong> You work 8:30 AM to 5:30 PM with two paid
    15-minute breaks and a 45-minute unpaid lunch. Clock time is 9 hours.
    Subtract only the lunch: 9:00 − 0:45 = <strong>8 hours 15 minutes</strong>
    (8.25 hours). Over a five-day week that's 41.25 hours, so in the US 1.25
    hours are federal overtime. If the employer also deducted the two rest
    breaks, you'd be paid for 7.75 hours a day: 2.5 hours a week less, and no
    overtime.
  </p>

  <h3>Watch out for automatic lunch deductions</h3>
  <p>
    Many time clock systems take 30 minutes off every shift automatically.
    That's fine on days you take a full, uninterrupted lunch. On days you
    work through it, or get called back early, the deduction is wrong and the
    time must be paid. If that happens, write it down and tell payroll, and
    check your weekly total against your own record.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculate hours with breaks</h2>
  <p>
    In the <a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/">Hours Worked
    Calculator</a> and the
    <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a>, enter
    only <em>unpaid</em> break minutes in the break field; leave paid rest
    breaks out. The <a href="/calculators/time-clock-calculator/">Time Clock
    Calculator</a> handles a lunch punch-out and punch-in directly. See also
    <a href="/guides/how-to-calculate-hours-worked/">how to calculate hours
    worked</a>.
  </p>

